  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Carter Center Access to Justice 2 and LEON - The Carter Center Access to Justice 2</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">The Carter Center Access to Justice 2 and LEON</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">Ambassaden har f&#xE5;tt en f&#xF6;rfr&#xE5;gan fr&#xE5;n Carter Center om f&#xF6;rnyat st&#xF6;d till programmet Access to Justice. Ambassaden kom &#xF6;verens med Carter Center att programf&#xF6;rslaget till ambassaden f&#xF6;r den nya fasen skulle inkludera en komponent f&#xF6;r att st&#xF6;dja utf&#xF6;randet av fria, transparenta och fredliga val. Val &#xE4;r en br&#xE4;nnande utmaning f&#xF6;r Liberia i &#xE5;r och Carter Center har tidigare erfarenhet av valst&#xF6;d i Liberia. F&#xF6;ljaktligen har Carter Center f&#xF6;reslagit att inom ramen f&#xF6;r denna insats inkludera och expandera sitt valarbete under fr&#xE4;mst 2017, med inriktning p&#xE5; medborgar- och v&#xE4;ljarutbildning, nationell valobservation samt f&#xF6;rebyggande av valrelaterat v&#xE5;ld. 

Det &#xF6;vergripande m&#xE5;let med insatsen &#xE4;r att st&#xE4;rka freden i Liberia genom att f&#xF6;rb&#xE4;ttra medborgares tillg&#xE5;ng till r&#xE4;ttvisa, st&#xE4;rka r&#xE4;ttss&#xE4;kerheten p&#xE5; l&#xE4;nsniv&#xE5; samt att verka f&#xF6;r fria, transparenta och s&#xE4;kra val. Carter Center har benat ut detta i fyra m&#xE5;l med tillh&#xF6;rande aktiviteter: 1) F&#xF6;rb&#xE4;ttra informationsspridning och medborgares delaktighet i och f&#xF6;rtroende f&#xF6;r valprocesser 2) &#xD6;ka tillg&#xE5;ng till r&#xE4;ttvisa genom att erbjuda gratis juridisk information och tvistl&#xF6;sningstj&#xE4;nster 3) St&#xE4;rka kapaciteten hos traditionella ledare att verka f&#xF6;r gott styre och lokalt r&#xE4;ttsut&#xF6;vande och 4) Ett f&#xF6;rb&#xE4;ttrat politiskt ramverk f&#xF6;r att &#xF6;ka m&#xF6;jligheterna till r&#xE4;ttslig pr&#xF6;vning p&#xE5; lokal niv&#xE5;. Ovanst&#xE5;ende m&#xE5;l uppn&#xE5;s bland annat genom st&#xF6;d till juridiska r&#xE5;dgivare p&#xE5; byniv&#xE5;, kapacitetsuppbyggnad av traditionella ledare och lokala strukturer, st&#xF6;d f&#xF6;r nationell valobservation p&#xE5; kort och l&#xE5;ng sikt, policyst&#xF6;d f&#xF6;r att harmonisera de olika r&#xE4;ttssystemen och st&#xF6;d till lokala tvistel&#xF6;sningsmekanismer. 

Insatsen syftar till att &#xF6;ka r&#xE4;ttss&#xE4;kerheten f&#xF6;r fattiga m&#xE4;n, kvinnor, pojkar och flickor i Liberia. &#xC5;teruppbyggnaden av det formella domstolsv&#xE4;sendet p&#xE5; landsbygden efter att n&#xE4;ra nog ha kollapsat under inb&#xF6;rdeskrigen, fortg&#xE5;r fr&#xE4;mst tack vare internationellt st&#xF6;d. Liberia har ett dubbelt r&#xE4;ttssystem med b&#xE5;de formella och sedvaner&#xE4;ttsliga domstolar. Bristen p&#xE5; kompetent personal, infrastruktur och resurser karakt&#xE4;riserar det formella r&#xE4;ttsv&#xE4;sendet som har en enorm eftersl&#xE4;pning. Traditionell sedvaner&#xE4;tt erk&#xE4;nns av konstitutionen och f&#xF6;rekommer i stora delar av landet. Den traditionella r&#xE4;ttsskipningen, under ledning av traditionella ledare eller by&#xE4;ldste, &#xE4;r oftast mer l&#xE4;ttillg&#xE4;nglig och effektiv men riskerar att leda till bristande respekt f&#xF6;r m&#xE4;nskliga r&#xE4;ttigheter i allm&#xE4;nhet och i synnerhet HBTQ-personers och kvinnors &#xE5;tnjutande av de m&#xE4;nskliga r&#xE4;ttigheterna. Studier visar att endast n&#xE5;gra f&#xE5; procent av alla r&#xE4;ttsfall n&#xE5;r det formella systemet. Carter Center arbetar d&#xE4;rf&#xF6;r med att st&#xE4;rka de mekanismer f&#xF6;r r&#xE4;ttsskipning som de facto hanterar de flesta r&#xE4;ttsfall och tvister. 

Programmet Access to Justice har under flera &#xE5;r arbetat med att bygga kapaciteten inom de sedvaner&#xE4;ttsliga strukturerna f&#xF6;r att bidra till &#xF6;kad kapacitet och m&#xF6;jlighet till ansvarsutkr&#xE4;vande samt att dessa strukturer &#xE4;r i linje med m&#xE4;nskliga r&#xE4;ttigheter. Insatsen syftar &#xE4;ven till att st&#xE4;rka l&#xE4;nken mellan de olika r&#xE4;ttssystemen. Medvetenhet och kunskap hos befolkningen om den formella r&#xE4;ttsprocessen &#xE4;r l&#xE5;g. Carter Center arbetar &#xE4;ven f&#xF6;r att informera befolkningen om landets lagar och medborgares juridiska r&#xE4;ttigheter, m&#xF6;jligheter och skyldigheter.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Embassy has received a request from the Carter Center for renewed support for the Access to Justice program. The Embassy agreed with the Carter Center that the draft proposal for the embassy for the new phase would include a component to support the execution of free, transparent and peaceful elections. Elections are a burning challenge for Liberia this year and Carter Center has previous experience of election support in Liberia. Consequently, the Carter Center has proposed to include and expand its electoral work in 2017, focusing on citizen and electoral education, national electoral observation and the prevention of electoral violence within the framework of this initiative.

The overall objective of the support is to strengthen Liberia&apos;s peace by enhancing citizens&apos; access to justice, strengthening the legal certainty at the county level and promoting free, transparent and safe elections. Carter Center has addressed this in four goals and associated activities: 1) Improve information dissemination and citizens involvement in and confidence in election processes 2) Increase access to justice by offering free legal information and dispute settlement services 3) Strengthen the capacity of traditional leaders to work for good governance and local law enforcement; and 4) an improved policy framework to increase opportunities for judicial review at local level. The above objectives are achieved, inter alia, through support for legal advisers, capacity building of traditional leaders and local structures, support for short and long-term national observation, policy support to harmonize the various legal systems and support for local dispute settlement mechanisms.

The support aims to increase legal certainty for poor men, women, boys and girls in Liberia. The reconstruction of the formal justice system in the countryside after almost collapsing during the civil war continues; thanks to international support. Liberia has a dual legal system with both formal and customary courts. The lack of competent personnel, infrastructure and resources characterizes the formal judicial system which has a huge lag. Traditional custom is recognized by the Constitution and occurs in most parts of the country. The traditional rule of law, led by traditional leaders, is usually more accessible and effective, but risks leading to inadequate respect for human rights in general and, in particular, the enjoyment of human rights by women and other minority groups. Studies show that only a small percent of all legal cases reach the formal system. Carter Center therefore works to strengthen the mechanisms of law enforcement that de facto handle most legal cases and disputes.

The Access to Justice program has for several years been working to build capacity within customary structures to contribute to increased capacity and ability to accountability, and that these structures are in line with human rights. The effort is also aimed at strengthening the link between the various legal systems. The consciousness and knowledge of the population about the formal legal process is low. Carter Center also works to inform the people about the laws and duties and rights of citizens under the country laws.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The overarching goal of the programme is &quot;Strengthening peace in Liberia by enhancing citizen ownership of government, strengthening the rule of law at the community, and preventing violence&quot;. During the period 2017 to 2021 this was implemented through four specific objectives: 

1. Improve information, citizen engagement, and confidence in a peaceful electoral process;
2. Provide effective free legal information and dispute resolution services;
3. Strengthen the capacity of traditional leaders to advance and implement good governance and community justice practices, and;
4. Support a policy framework to increase access to justice at the community level.

Objective 1 contributed to to safe, inclusive and fair elections through civic education, national long-term and short-term observation of the election and violence prevention, by mobilising a diverse range of influential community actors to conduct civic education and to provide support to community violence prevention mechanisms, as well as by deploying short- and long-term election observers through Liberia Election Observation Network (LEON). In 2021, The Carter Center entered into a new agreement with Sida to continue supporting LEON and their work with election observation in the contribution &quot;Carter Center support to LEON 2021-2024&quot; (Activity-ID: SE-0-SE-6-14828A0101-LBR-15150). 

Objective 2-4 was implemented through the The Access to Justice programme and has contributed to access to justice for all and improving local good governance and conflict resolution. During the period 2017-2021 this comprised work with customary and local mechanisms offering free legal advice and conflict mediation services; strengthening the capacity of traditional leaders to promote good governance and community justice practices and; improve the national policy framework to support Access to Justice at the Community level. During this period, the Carter Center started working with Community Justice Advisors - volunteers supporting community members with assistance to justice seekers, case assistance and community awareness raising. 

In 2021, the support was extended until 2023, for continued work with the Community Justice Advisors component, with the following Sub-Objectives: 

1. Increased rural and urban marginalized populations understanding of their rights and responsibilities within the justice systems.
2. Increased provision and utilization of needed legal support for marginalized citizens to access justice

The extension June 2023 - December 2025 will only cover The Carter Center&apos;s proposed exit strategy for their Rule of Law programme in Liberia - according to the following objectives: 

Objective 1: To support the establishment and organizational capacity of a new Liberian civil society organization able to receive direct funding from Sida and other international partners.

Objective 2: Increase access to justice for underserved populations in Liberia</narrative>
  </description>
